      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "We report a case of a 47-year-old woman with a typical clinical pain, a ST elevation in lateral EKG leads and an increase of the CPK-MB enzymes serum level suggesting the diagnosis of an acute lateral myocardial infarction. The emergency coronary arteriography did not showed any coronary artery lesions but a coronary artery fistula from the left main coronary artery to a terminal thrombosed aneurysmal dilatation located between the left atrium and the right pulmonary artery. Through this case report, authors will discuss aetiology, physiopathology, clinical presentation and treatment of coronary artery fistulae."},
